  "checkpoints": [
    {
      "afterStep": 6,
      "question": "height[l]=0, and the tallest left wall seen so far is 1. What happens here?",
      "options": [
        "Nothing traps \u2014 height[l] becomes the new left_max",
        "Water traps here, up to the left_max"
      ],
      "answer": "Water traps here, up to the left_max",
      "why": "This bar is shorter than the tallest wall already seen on the left, so water sits on top of it up to that wall's height."
    },
    {
      "afterStep": 10,
      "question": "height[l]=2 and height[r]=1. Which side gets processed next?",
      "options": [
        "l, the taller side",
        "r, the shorter side"
      ],
      "answer": "r, the shorter side",
      "why": "The side with the lower current bar is the one whose water level is already decided \u2014 the far side is guaranteed at least as tall, so it can't be the limiting wall."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The water sitting above any one bar is limited by the shorter of the tallest wall to its left and the tallest wall to its right. You don't need both of those exact heights everywhere at once, though.",
    "Whichever side currently has the lower running wall (tallest-so-far on the left vs. tallest-so-far on the right) is the side whose water level is already decided \u2014 the far side is guaranteed to be at least as tall, so it can't be the limiting wall.",
    "Keep a marker at each end, and track the tallest wall seen so far from each side. Compare those two running tallest walls; process whichever side has the shorter one \u2014 either raise that side's running tallest wall, or, if the current bar is shorter than it, add the difference as trapped water. Move that marker inward and repeat until the markers meet."
  ],
